 <office:body>
  <office:text text:use-soft-page-breaks="true">
   <office:forms form:automatic-focus="false" form:apply-design-mode="false"/>
   <text:sequence-decls>
    <text:sequence-decl text:display-outline-level="0" text:name="Illustration"/>
    <text:sequence-decl text:display-outline-level="0" text:name="Table"/>
    <text:sequence-decl text:display-outline-level="0" text:name="Text"/>
    <text:sequence-decl text:display-outline-level="0" text:name="Drawing"/>
    <text:sequence-decl text:display-outline-level="0" text:name="Figure"/>
   </text:sequence-decls>
   <text:h text:style-name="P91" text:outline-level="2">Chapter 1</text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="P4"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T198">A blink of the eye and I find myself lying on a hard stone floor instead of lounging on my sofa. My fingers brush against warm silver inlaid in the stone floor as I get to my feet and see that the silver forms some arcane diagram. Three other people stand with me inside the circle while another half dozen surround us. Those three, two men and another woman, look like typical 20 somethings but the other six are shadowy robed figures that better fit the solid stone dungeon around us.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P4"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T199">So do you guys know what’s going on?” I ask.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P4"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T199">One of the men just grunts out ‘huh’ but the woman replies, “Where even are we? How did we get here?”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P4"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T200">Magic is the obvious answer but that makes me realize that I cannot feel any mana in the room. Even without a summoning ritual powerful enough to pull four people across the world I should be able to feel something. My mana sense has had centuries of practice so there is something wrong here. Well, more than the obvious.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P1"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T6">“</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T7">We’re in an isekai!” gasps the second man. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T8">It is possible that this is another world, but I don’t know why he used the japanese word. “</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9">Oh my god, we’ve been summoned to fight the demon lord with our cheat powers!” the guy gushes in an impressive leap of logic.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P1"><text:span text:style-name="T9"><text:tab/>“</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T10">You’re mostly correct.” said one of the robed people surrounding us </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T11">to my surprise. He goes on to talk about a whole lot of nothing. There are no demon lords instead dungeons just appear and start spawning monsters in this world. We have been empowered to cull them so that we can fulfill our duty to protect our innocent kidnappers. More or less.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P6"><text:tab/>“Well, these dungeons sound fascinating, but can’t you do your own fighting?” I ask.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P6"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T201">Of course, we can! You’re only here to destroy the cores that are otherwise invulnerable.” He glares, “You don’t need to worry. We understand how soft you’re previous world was. Just follow orders and we will keep you safe.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P6"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T202">Not all of us are weak! Just give us an opportunity and we can show you what we are capable of!” declares one of the men in the circle complete with a raised fist. He is the same one that was excited about fighting a demon lord. I do not want to see what he is capable of.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P6"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T203">With that dubious pledge we are shepherded away for dinner. My fellow victims are much hungrier than me until I see that most of the food is meat. It’s not quite a feast but close enough for me. The robed man leaving is another pleasant surprise. An older teen girl joins us instead.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P6"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T204">Greetings Heroes! I’m Merin!” chirps the girl, “I’m looking forward to working together with all of you!”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P6"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T205">I’m Arthur and I’m excited too.” says the man that is actually excited to be here. Noah and Alex introduce themselves as well.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P6"><text:soft-page-break/><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T206">I’m Kim and I’m not happy about being kidnapped.” I say.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P6"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T206">Why are you such a killjoy?” Arthur complains, “It’s not Merin’s fault so can’t you just be happy?” My only reply is to stare at him before turning to the food. Honestly, playing along with these people probably is a good idea but it rubs me the wrong way. I would have to stay human for the entire time for one thing. It is better to make my displeasure plain so I can see how people react.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P7"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T207">It’s alright, this is a hard change but she will adapt.” Merin says, “Unfortunately, there is no way for us to warn people before we summon them.” That’s not the issue but there’s no point trying to convince her either. Instead, I shrug take a backseat for the rest of the evening. Arthur and Merin talk but the other two stay silent as well.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P8"><text:tab/>The suite we retire to has a common area with separate bedrooms for all five of us. Overall its sparse but clean and functional. More than I expected for conscripts. <text:span text:style-name="T208">The bed is not great but still comfortable enough to flop onto. It is time to take stock of this situation starting with the fact that this is not my body.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P8"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T209">I look like a 20 something year old Japanese woman with pure white hair. While that is typical the reality is that I am a kitsune, a literal fox that gained magic. Shape shifting is my most used ability but this is different. I can feel the separation between myself and this body despite how unused to spiritual possession I am. There is even something odd and mechanical in here as well but no other soul. No, this body was prepared just for me and there is only one person I know likely responsible. Hekate.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P6"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T210">A warning would have been nice but it is reassure that the goddess I follow was involved in the summoning. It is still more consideration than I got from Inari. Now is not the time to get drawn into the past. There is a whole world to explore and no reason to wait until tomorrow to start.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P6"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T211">The lights are dim and no one is present when I leave my room. While I avoid making more noise than necessary this is not the time for sneaking. This is just a midnight stroll and I am simply unaware of any possible curfew. The lack of guards in the hall might as well be an implicit approval of my actions.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P5"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T211">The walls are strange here. All seamless stone but not concrete. The grain is visible and it looks like someone molded the whole building out of a solid piece. The glowing crystals in the wall are probably magic so maybe there magic rock molding here too. No cracks either so it is over built or magically reinforced. Now that I think about it the air here is way more comfortable than I would expect from a stone building. I wish my magic sense was not stifled so I could get a feel for the magic at play.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P5"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T212">None of the doors are open and prying would be odd for a simple stroll so it does not take long to scout out the buildings. It is larger than I would expect if we are the only here but not by much. The closed rooms could easily contain a few other people or purpose built chambers. The large door leading outside is the only one I am willing to try. Tonight is a new moon leaving the world dark but enough of </text:span><text:soft-page-break/><text:span text:style-name="T212">those light crystal are around to see the outline of a walled in courtyard. Thick wooden gates block the area in but there is no much else around. Ladders leading up to the ramparts let me get onto the walls.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P5"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T213">The sight from the ramparts is a relief I did not know I needed. In the distances is the silhouette of a forest. That I can work with once I fix my magic. I can even pay a visit to Hekate from there. It will make a lovely little home and one right next to a town.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P5"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T214">There is another wall in the distance hiding the faint glow of a sleeping town. At least we were not summoned by some remote cult but why keep us distant? There are no guards here so it is not a safety issue. The only reasons I can think of are to keep us from annoying the town or getting attached to it. I doubt this is a good sign.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:h text:style-name="P82" text:outline-level="1">Chapter 2</text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="P66"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T6">“</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T12">What are you doing?” Alex asks when she enters the common area the next morning to find me staring at my hand as it clenches and uncle.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P66"><text:span text:style-name="T12"><text:tab/>I have been awake for at least an hour and had time to poke around the magic entwined with this body. The barrier surrounding me lets magical energy, mana, in but not out. I don’t think it was designed as a prison for me but it might as well be. I simply do not have the leverage to even try breaking through. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T13">That is not the only thing in here with me. There is an extensive network of mana woven throughout this body like some kind of fungus. I’m not sure what purpose it serves but it does give me an idea.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P66"><text:span text:style-name="T13"><text:tab/>I can do the same. It is not like there is any mind or soul in this body to oppose me. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T14">Binding myself to a new body is not a trivial thing so I started with a single hand. That how I found out that the magic fungus is some kind of symbiote tied into damn near every bodily function. It does a great job of blending in with the biology but now my spirit is brushing against it directly and it is the weirdest sensation. At least it does not object to me but this thing is going to get in my way.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P9"><text:tab/>I’m not going to explain any of that to Alex so I say, “Something feels different.”<text:span text:style-name="T215">Alex’s expression gets more incredulous. “So, did you get any sleep?”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P9"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T216">Some.” She replies but does not elaborate.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P9"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T216">That’s good. I don’t think we can trust these people. They basically conscripted us and are trying to present it as an honor. That’s not the action of friends, its the action of an abuser. It’s important to get what rest we can if we’re going to deal with this.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P1"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T6">“</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T15">What do you ‘we’re going to deal with this?’” Alex asks with narrowed eyes. I guess she is not interested in rocking the boat.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P11"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T217">They went to the trouble of bringing us here, they’ve got plans for us</text:span>.” I say, “I don’t know what hoops they have for us to jump through, but a lack of sleep won’t help.”</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P11"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T218">Right.” she replies before sitting down and looking away.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P11"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T218">Noah is the next to wake up and I greet, “Good morning! Did you sleep well?”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P11"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T219">Like a log!” he boasts, “Gotta be prepared for the excitement!”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P11"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T220">You’re looking forward to whatever is in store for us?”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P11"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T220">Well yeah, they’re calling us heroes. This has got to be a pretty nice gig, right?”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P11"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T220">He sounds confident so I push back a little to see how firm that confidence is. “But we haven’t done anything heroic yet.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P11"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T220">Don’t worry, we will. The magic wouldn’t have picked us if we weren’t heroes after all.” He reassures. That is not the response I was expecting. It is also false with me here. I am not hero material.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P11"><text:soft-page-break/><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T221">Wait, how do you know that?” Alex asks.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P11"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T221">Well, it’s obvious right?” Noah replies, “When I thought about it last night, I realized the must be some selection process. It would be ridiculous to just summon up random people. How would you know if they are any good at what you wanted? Merin was so confident that we were heroes, too. It’s just proof that they knew they would summon heroes.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P11"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T221">My jaw drops a little. It always amazes me to watch someone ignore obvious alternatives. Merin could simply be lying or lied to. You do not need to worry about summon random people if you are planning to beat them into submission anyway. If they cared about skill or consent recruiting from the local population would be better.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P11"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T222">I don’t think I can be a hero.” Alex whispers.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P11"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T223">Don’t worry, everyone has the potential to be a hero.” Noah reassures to no effect.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P11"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T224">After Merin shows up we wake up Arthur for breakfast. He is not a morning person and the lack of coffee does not help. Once we are all fed and seated back in the suite Merin announces she has a lesson for us.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P11"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T225">Alright, it is time to introduce you to the most important aspect of our world.” Merin says, “The System.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P11"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T225">Arthur perks up at that and shouts, “I knew it wasn’t just a dream!”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P12"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T226">Uh, anyway, you can access it by saying ‘status’ with the intention of seeing your own information.” Merin prompts and the instructions actually work.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13"><text:tab/>A little screen pops up as if there was computer in front of me. Strength, Dexterity, Constitution, Endurance, Willpower and Intelligence are all marked down as 10. I do not know what that means but a wild guess says it is not great. [Blessing of Hekate] is listed under skills so the Goddess did send me here. This is a cute little System thing and I assume the source of the magic fungus in here with me.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P12"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T227">Once we all finished poking around the status screen Merin continues, “The System is the key to power. You won’t be able to get anywhere in this world without it. Fortunately, it is easy to use. Your stats are split into three categories: physical, defensive, and mental. Each of those categories has a power stat and a control stat. Uh, let’s see.” she mumbles to herself for a second, “Right, magic is governed by mental stats and Endurance governs recovery of health and stamina. That should be all the tricky bits.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P10"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T228">And we fight monsters to gain levels?” Arthur grins.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P10"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T228">Mostly, you gain levels by absorbing the cores form monsters.” Merin said.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P10"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T228">Oh, so this System just hands out raw power to people with power?” I chuckle, “What a terrible idea. I don’t want to play that game.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P10"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T229">What?” Merin asks perplexed.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P10"><text:soft-page-break/><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T229">The System is giving power to people who already have power. It’s just a rat race, gaining power to get more power.” I say, “It’s an endless tread mill that just paints a target on your back as soon as you get on. No thanks.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P10"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T229">You would rather be powerless.” Arthur scowls, “We all have to pull our weight, Kimberly. A pair of tits is no excuse.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P10"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T230">Alex and Merin tense while I respond, “You’re an ass, Arthur, but my name’s not Kimberly.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P10"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T231">Then what is it?”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P10"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T231">You can call me Kim.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P10"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T231">But what is it short for?” Arthur demands.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P10"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T231">You can call me Kim.” I repeat and look him dead in the eyes.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P10"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T232">Noah puts a hand on Arthurs shoulder and says, “She’s in a mood, let’s just move on.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P3"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T232">Right, yes,” Merin says jumping on the chance to get things back on track, “Monsters. They are serious threat since dungeons constantly produce them. Even worse, dungeons quickly grow in strength producing stronger and stronger monsters if left alone. That is why it’s our job to keep them under control.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P68"><text:span text:style-name="T6"><text:tab/>She continues to explain in vivid detail all of the damage that monsters can do. All it does is convince me I want no part of this war. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T16">Adopting some stranger’s enemies is not a good plan. A good plan is getting access to the standard abilities of the kitsune. Foxfire, illusions, and shapeshifting.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P14"><text:tab/>Summoning ethereal flames is not going to happen while this barrier keeps my mana internal. Illusions work just fine as I can create the sensation of phantom heat and cold in my hand. External illusion have the same problem as foxfire. <text:span text:style-name="T233">Trying to shift the nail of one of my fingers into a claw is painful. I can feel something tear and I have to bite back a cry. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T234">I think I ripped part of the System.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P14"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T234">What did you do?” Alex asks while watching me nurse my finger.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P68"><text:span text:style-name="T16"><text:tab/>“</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T17">I wanted to see how this System affects my magic.” I frown, “The result was not great.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P15"><text:tab/>“Bullsh…” she trails off as the gears of her mind turn. The warring emotions play across her face.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P15"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T235">Don’t bother trying non-System magic. It never works.” Merin says, “Often it hurts the caster as you found out.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:h text:style-name="P83" text:outline-level="1">Chapter 3</text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="P69"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T18">It proved a little tricky but I got my shapeshifting to work last night. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T19">Illusions work on just about anything interactive and the System is no exception. Even better both the System and my illusions are magical constructs. There is no difference between myself and an illusion of myself </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T20">in this case. The System does not care about how impossibly contorted my body is as long as it can pretend that body is human.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P69"><text:span text:style-name="T20"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T21">That infuriating mana barrier is still in place though. It follows the shape of the illusion which means I can control it size. Even though I can uncover parts of my body, my spirit is still trapped inside. The feeling of controlling my hand purely through nerves is weird </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T22">after so long as a spirit</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T21">.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P69"><text:span text:style-name="T21"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T23">The day takes a turn for the worse when Merin leads us to the courtyard after breakfast. There we meet Randel a beast of a man with the physique of a body builder. He is out drill sergeant and it is time for physical training. </text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P2"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T236">God damn it.” I hiss. The fact I did not see this coming just adds insult to injury.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P2"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T237">Have something to say, recruit?” Randel challenges.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P70"><text:span text:style-name="T24"><text:tab/>“</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T25">No.” I sigh </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T27">and prepare for an annoying day</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T25">.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T26"> </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T27">Physical training</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T26"> is nigh useless for a shapeshifter. Even the muscle memory is questionable </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T27">since</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T26"> it </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T27">is tied to a specific form</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T26">. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T27">At least I have enough skill to avoid the soreness and exhaustion. It is still not worth the trouble.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P2"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T238">How the hell are you still going?” Arthur demands after we do some number of sprints. I zoned out at some point.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P70"><text:span text:style-name="T24"><text:tab/>“</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T28">Huh?</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T29">” This is a little awkward. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T28">Everyone else is ready to collapse and I have no convenient excuse. “I guess I got in a good rhythm.” Nobody buys it.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P70"><text:span text:style-name="T28"><text:tab/>“</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T30">You cheated!” Arthur glares.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P70"><text:span text:style-name="T30"><text:tab/>“</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T31">Kim</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T30">, what is your endurance?” Randel asks.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P16"><text:tab/>It takes me a moment to figure out what he is asking before answering, “10.” There is no point in an unconvincing lie.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P16"><text:tab/>“So you do have a cheat ability then.” Randel muses. My surprise prompts him to chuckle, “It’s not common but you aren’t the first to have an ability that works with the System.”</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P16"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T239">What the hell!?” Arthur complains, “What did she do to deserve an ability!”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P70"><text:span text:style-name="T30"><text:tab/>“</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T31">You’ll just have to put in your own hard work then.” Randel says, “However, it does make the training too easy and prevents stat increases. Turn it off and get back to work.” I consider telling him that it is a permanent effect but he does not need to know that. The rest of day is pure torture. Even when we get back to the suite everything is pain.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P17"><text:soft-page-break/><text:tab/>There is no way I am staying here for another day even if I have to hide out in the forest for a while. Still I want full use of my magic if for no other reason than to get out of this world and visit Hekate. It is time to throw every idea at this damn barrier and see what sticks. </text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P17"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T240">A couple of h</text:span>ours pass as I try to figure out which part of the System is creating the barrier before I just try removing the System it from a spot within my hand in frustration. Naturally it is the dumbest one that finally gets a result. <text:span text:style-name="T240">A new barrier forms in the void despite the illusion being seamless. Something else is creating the barrier and my System is just marking the area to include.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P70"><text:span text:style-name="T30"><text:tab/>“</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T32">Hah, I got it!” I shout as a small ethereal flame hovers above my open palm. Adding a simple illusion to mimic the System is all I needed. Although I guess the barrier is really just the boundary of some global effect.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P67"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T6">“</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T33">How do you have magic now!?” Arthur replies.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P18"><text:tab/>“I always had magic, I just had to figure out how to get it to play nice with the System.”</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P18"><text:tab/>“Bullshit, Earth didn’t have magic.”</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P18"><text:tab/>I roll my eyes, “Technology scales better and isn’t limited by imagination. It out competed magic but magic is still around.”</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P67"><text:span text:style-name="T33"><text:tab/>“I don’t believe you!” Arthur declares, “You must have made a deal with some kind of monster. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T34">You’ve been against us this entire time. You’re just trying to lure us into a trap!”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P19"><text:tab/>I sigh and rub my temples. This guy has lost it but his ‘facts’ are close enough to be believable. I would like to bring these guys back home, except Arthur, but that offer will seem sketchy now. Well, people believe what they want to believe so let the chips fall where they may. “Alright, you’ve clearly gotten lost in your own imagination” I stand up from my seat, “You are at least a little right, I do want to ditch this place. Am I the only one? Is no one else interested in going home?” I look toward Noah and Alex but neither will meet my eyes. Arthur looks smug.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P71"><text:span text:style-name="T34"><text:tab/>“And how do you plan on going home without </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T35">help from the Empire?</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T34">” </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T35">Merin asks.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P20"><text:tab/>“I have my ways, but are you actually interested in leaving?”</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P71"><text:span text:style-name="T35"><text:tab/>“What? Of course, not!” Merin rejects, “</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T36">And if you keep talking about this I will have to tell Randel.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P21"><text:tab/>“Well, I guess that settles things then.” I say.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P21"><text:tab/>“No, if you’re going to be a part of this team then you should teach us magic.” Arthur says.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P21"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T241">Hold on, why are you bringing us into this?” Alex asks.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P21"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T241">It could be neat to have magic.” Noah shrugs.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P21"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T241">Why would she even agree?”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P21"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T241">Well, she wouldn’t want Randel to think she isn’t a team player after all.” Arthur says.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P21"><text:soft-page-break/><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T241">It really is amazing how fast some people will jump on a perceived weakness. I snap my fingers but throw in a small illusion for effect. A soft but deep boom rolls through the room with a slight echo. It takes a moment for people to figure out where the sound came from but everyone’s attention is locked on me now. “You’re the type of person that is quick to resort to blackmail, Arthur. That’s good to know.” I say, “I will not be teaching you anything.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P21"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T241">Hold on, that’s not fair!” Arthur argues, “I’m just pointing out facts.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P21"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T241">That is a pretty heavy accusation.” Noah agrees, “Maybe you’re being a little harsh, Kim.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P21"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T241">I’ve stated my opinion, Noah. You don’t have to agree with it.” I say and walk to my room.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:h text:style-name="P84" text:outline-level="1">Chapter 4</text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="P72"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T37">Once I feel enough time has passed I go to the door of my room and layer an illusion over it. The illusion is a simple copy of the light hitting a point on the opposite. In effect its a makeshift peephole and it shows that the main room is empty.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P22"><text:tab/>As soon as the knob turns the door pushes into the room <text:span text:style-name="T242">from Merin leaning against it. I did not consider checking that blind spot of the peephole. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T243">The girl takes only a moment to get over her surprise before jumping to her feet.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P22"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T243">Odd place for a nap.” I quip.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P22"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T244">I did it in case you tried something.” Merin says, “It’s a good thing I did. Why don’t you go back to sleep before we all get in trouble?”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P22"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T244">Collective punishment.” The phrase taste terrible, “That’s just more reason to leave. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T245">Maybe you should think about it too.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P72"><text:span text:style-name="T37"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T38">Merin sighs and closes her eyes. “You have a duty to fulfill.” she intones with an ethereal voice. An unnatural sense of guilt settles over me. “A lot effort has been put in to bring you here and you wouldn’t want to spit in the face of the Empire would you? Return to bed.” The thought of going back to sleep appears in my mind with a sense of contentment.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P23"><text:tab/>The fake emotions are a far more effective mind control than I ever expected from Merin. Although, I do not expect much mind control from humans in the first place. My first impulse is to further separate the system from my mind but I hesitate. The foreign guilt wants me to think it’s a bad idea but it has found a good reason to latch onto. This is probably not the last time I will face some kind of mental intrusion and ripping out the System is too heavy handed of a solution. After encountering creatures with enough willpower to see through the mental components of my illusion I stopped using them but I don’t need to worry about that if I am the target.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P72"><text:span text:style-name="T38"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T39">Illusory revulsion floods my mind making it easy to ignore Merin’s manipulations. It is unfocused since I cannot feel the intrusion but this is good enough for a hack job. I can see a lot of potential in this.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P24"><text:tab/>“Actually, I would like to spit in the face of the Empire. They had no respect for me when dragging me here and the feeling is mutual.” I state to the shock of Merin. The revulsion might be a little strong.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P24"><text:tab/>Merin’s face twists in fury before she raises a hand. I may not be much of a fighter but dodging a slap is not hard. Despite that Merin exclaims, “You dodged! <text:span text:style-name="T246">Your place has already been decided. Stop resisting, it only makes things harder.</text:span>”</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P24"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T246">If you find it too hard, there’s no need to fight.” I say and back into the room, “I don’t really see why you guys even care so much in the first place.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P24"><text:soft-page-break/><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T247">Everyone has their place in society and only by working together can we achieve our potential.” Merin says as </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T248">she advances, “You will learn that in time.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P72"><text:span text:style-name="T39"><text:tab/>“</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T40">I see.” I frown. It’s not surprise that Merin has bought into the propaganda but it is still disappointing. There is no way out without a fight so I close my eyes. A brilliant strobing light beams into Merin’s face causing her to reel back and cover her face. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T41">As soon as I get out of the room I throw an invisibility illusion on. It is the same as the peephole illusion and far from perfect but it’</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T42">s</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T41"> good enough.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P72"><text:span text:style-name="T41"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T42">Instead of dealing with the main gate I head to the same ramparts as the first day and hop over the wall </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T43">before I can think about it</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T42">. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T43">The landing is painful enough that I need to stifle my scream but at least there are no broken bones. Numbing the pain would be easy but pain has a purpose and that stunt should be painful.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P72"><text:span text:style-name="T43"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T2">“You gain the ability [Pain Resistance]!”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P25"><text:tab/>Following the odd text message appearing in my vision the pain of torn muscles recedes. It does not lessen but instead becomes less insistent. A moment passes before I realize the System is working on the one part of my body it is still attached to, the mind. While that has potential I focus on fixing my legs and running for the forest.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P72"><text:span text:style-name="T43"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T44">Once I reach my goal I slow down. Without my own noise to cover them up the sound of rapid footsteps is clear. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T45">That is unfortunate for the both of us since I turn in a metaphorical direction and drag my pursuer with me into liminal space. Technically liminal spaces are the edge of reality and border everywhere but my affinity is only high enough with forest to cross over easily since I am a forest creature. Since these space are more conceptual than physical they are great for traveling to distant or even otherwise impossible to reach locations. Assuming you know how to navigate them that is.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P72"><text:span text:style-name="T45"><text:tab/>“</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T46">So what’s the next step in your plan?” Randel questions now that he caught up to me.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P26"><text:tab/>“You should worry more about your plan.” I say as I turn around and to see a generic forest stretching beyond the edge we just crossed. Liminal spaces respond to expectations and Randel might have been able to drag me out of the forest if I didn’t just close the door.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P72"><text:span text:style-name="T46"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T47">He quirks an eyebrow and scoffs, “You think you’re the first otherworlder to run away? Even if you managed to sneak out properly tracking your presence through this forest would be easy. Even your invisibility didn’t matter. Now if you return quietly and tell me who you were going to meet then your punishment will be lighter.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P72"><text:span text:style-name="T47"><text:tab/>I forgot about the invisibility since he is looking straight at me regardless. This is going to complicate things but invisibility might still block details. “I guess it doesn’t really matter if I tell you. I’m going to meet Hekate, but why did you follow me? </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T48">If you could track me down later why did you rush here?”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P27"><text:soft-page-break/><text:tab/>Randel gives me a look of pity, “You don’t get it. You are a tool of the Empire. Insubordination is one thing but if I let you go you would be labeled a traitor. I know you won’t understand now, but this is for your own good.”</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P72"><text:span text:style-name="T48"><text:tab/>“</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T49">No, it’s not for my own good. You don’t even care about me beyond being a tool for your empire. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T50">You really are just a tool of the Empire</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T49">.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T48">” I say and add the illusion of a heat haze surrounding me. My location is already revealed so I hope the distortion will let me dodge if he tries to grab me. I only need a second of distraction to slip away in a liminal space.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P72"><text:span text:style-name="T48"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T49">Randel lunges as soon as I try to take a step back. Since sneaking away did not work it is time to try the opposite. I crouch and dive toward his legs causing his grapple to miss as he trips over me. His yelp of surprise confirms my illusions are partially working. The confusion gives me long enough to dart behind a tree and let Randel fall from my mind. It is ‘out of mind, out of sight’ here. Despite traveling in a circle there is no one else here with me.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P72"><text:span text:style-name="T48"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T50">The rest of the trip is much easier especially with foxfire flares lightning the dark of the night now that stealth is unneeded. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T51">Then again stealth didn’t do much in the first place. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T52">This was not one of my better escapes. At least I did not need to use shapeshifting so there was still some wiggle room.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P25"><text:tab/></text:p>
   <text:h text:style-name="P85" text:outline-level="1">Chapter 5</text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="P73"><text:span text:style-name="T6"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T53">The transition from forest into Hekate’s garden is hard to miss. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T54">While the tree cover thins and the variety of plants increase, the path </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T55">is the giveaway.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T54"> Everyone enters the garden on a path </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T55">although the twists and turns make it hard to see ahead. Plants of all kinds line the path but never encroach on the main path. Every so often other paths will branch off, some distinct while others are just thin patches in the surrounding plants but none of them are as clear as the main path. At least not at first.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P73"><text:span text:style-name="T55"><text:tab/>The first time I stumbled in here I was looking for a safe place to hide. The borders between a well worn path and the wilderness seemed like a good enough spot. It was odd when I tried to leave the path only to end up on another one. Several days and dozens of branching paths later, I discovered that </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T56">forwards and backwards are the only direction in the garden</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T55">. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T56">I should have accepted it at that point too.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P73"><text:span text:style-name="T56"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T57">The center of the garden is a simple cottage adorned with witch stuff. Dried herbs and baubles hang from the eaves. A gnarly broom rests against the wall. There is even bubbling cauldron with some green liquid belching colorful smoke. I have never seen any of it used but Hekate is the goddess of witchcraft. She has an image to maintain and I guess this helps.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P28"><text:tab/>Entering the cottage I announce, “Kate, I’m back.”</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P73"><text:span text:style-name="T57"><text:tab/>“</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T58">Decided to pop in for a visit did you?” Hekate says with a plate of fresh cookies scenting the air. That brings up mixed feelings. On one hand there are peanut butter cookies. On the other hand she expects to need peanut butter cookies on hand. Hekate is using her grandmother face today. “</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T59">Quit working yourself up and sit already.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P29"><text:tab/>“Yes, I was quite surprised to find myself in banished again. I don’t suppose you know anything about that?” I quip after sitting down.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P29"><text:tab/>“Not banished, transmigrated.”</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P29"><text:tab/>“Transmigrated? But I didn’t die.”</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P30"><text:tab/>“You’re still a soul stuffed in a new body. It’s close enough. That’s what that summoning spell does. Pluck a soul from the Styx, craft a new body for it and glue everything together.” That explanation has a lot of implications, “<text:span text:style-name="T249">I don’t think that spell was ever supposed to be used so often but there is another matter. You were followed.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P30"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T249">Is that a problem?”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P74"><text:span text:style-name="T59"><text:tab/>“</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T60">No, it’s a dilemma.” Hekate says and things click into place. Hekate is the goddess of magic but she is also associated with crossroads. Often times she is depicted with a torch and now it looks like I overlooked something and need enlightenment.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P74"><text:span text:style-name="T60"><text:tab/>“</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T61">Is it because Randel might escape at some point?” I guess.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P74"><text:span text:style-name="T61"><text:tab/>“He’s stuck there unless you decide to rescue him. The issue is that you can’t hurt him.” Hekate grins at my bewildered look, “You’re dealing with an Empire, dear, they are obsessed with control and </text:span><text:soft-page-break/><text:span text:style-name="T61">ruled by fear. A slave running away is something they can understand. The soldier sent to retrieve her disappearing without a trace? The paranoia is going to go wild from that. They know you can’t hurt him and the idea the he just got lost in the woods is ridiculous.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P74"><text:span text:style-name="T61"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T62">I shove a cookie in my face to wash away my annoyance of Empires. “That’s not much of a dilemma though. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T63">There is no way I won’t end up labeled an enemy.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T62">”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P74"><text:span text:style-name="T62"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T64">Hekate sighs, “There is a complication. They’re going to think someone is poaching heroes and take measures to prevent that from happening again. If you abandon Randel, then you will also abandon Alex, Noah, and Arthur.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P31"><text:tab/>Oh. Arthur doesn’t matter but I don’t want to burn those bridges. An entire world to explore and no one to share the journey with would suck. Everyone else is likely tied to the Empire either for or against it. I don’t want to get drawn that deeply into the fight.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P31"><text:tab/>Can I even do anything about it? The only reason I got away from Randel the first time is that I can navigate liminal space and he can’t. Rescuing him would mean returning to real space. <text:span text:style-name="T250">My capture is all but guaranteed. Freedom or Friends: That is a hell of a dilemma. I lean back to let the situation sink in while munching on another cookie.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P31"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T250">This isn’t working.” I say once getting tired of beating my head against the wall, “What am I even doing? Neither of these futures even exist! They’re just illusion! This is a false choice. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T251">I can make my own choice.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T250">”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P74"><text:span text:style-name="T64"><text:tab/>“</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T65">Oh? The possibilities are endless, of course, but those two </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T66">are the best I see.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T65"> By all means see if you find one that leads to a better place.” Hekate chuckles.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P74"><text:span text:style-name="T62"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T66">I sigh because know she’s right but I still have do it myself. Let’s see, I don’t care about Randel so the choice isn’t whether or not to save him, it’s whether or not to abandon the heroes. I already offered them the choice to join me and they turned it down. Kidnapping them would go poorly for a variety of reasons, and I have no leverage on the Empire to stop their efforts aside from Randel. Protecting them from brainwashing is the only other option.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P32"><text:tab/>Preventing magical manipulation isn’t impossible. It would even help persuade them to leave the Empire. Something about this feels wrong but regardless I ask, “Would you help me protect their minds?”</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P32"><text:tab/>“Sure, but what are you protecting them from?” Hekate asks.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P32"><text:tab/>“From whatever the Empire is going to do.”</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P32"><text:tab/>“Well if you want to enthrall them that’s certainly doable, but that’s not a request I expected.” Hekate says with feigned shock.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P74"><text:span text:style-name="T66"><text:tab/>“</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T67">What?”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P33"><text:tab/>“Not everything is magic.”</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P74"><text:soft-page-break/><text:span text:style-name="T67"><text:tab/>“But </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T68">that…” I sag in my seat, “They want to be part of the Empire? Why?”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P34"><text:tab/>“Stability, dear. People will put up with a lot as long as it is stable. Whatever else it may be, the Empire is stable otherwise it wouldn’t exist.”</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P34"><text:tab/>“Oh. Oh no. There is no middle ground is there? It really is black or white. I’m either a part of the Empire or an enemy to everyone in it.” my head meets the table.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P74"><text:span text:style-name="T68"><text:tab/>“Yep, getting captured rescuing Randel is a critical part of </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T69">that</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T68"> path. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T69">The man actually does care enough to protect the Heroes under him. That’s important but the gap between you and them will only widen from your absence.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T68">”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P74"><text:span text:style-name="T68"><text:tab/>“</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T69">It’s not worth it.” I </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T70">grimace at the thought of trying to navigate the Empire. Discipline is not something I care for. It’s probably for the best. I can pretend to be human but there is no true place for a kitsune. I would have to worry about betrayal from anyone I told but I’m tired of wearing a mask around ‘friends.’ This is as good a time as any for change. Huh, all of the cookies disappeared.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P74"><text:span text:style-name="T70"><text:tab/>“</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T71">Do you need more, dear, or would you like to move on to the next topic?”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P74"><text:span text:style-name="T71"><text:tab/>“</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T74">Alright</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T73">, what did you send me to </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T75">another world</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T73"> for?</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T72">”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P74"><text:span text:style-name="T72"><text:tab/>“</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T74">To get the both </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T75">you and the world</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T74"> out of </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T75">a</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T74"> rut.” Hekate says and pauses to give me an expectant look. I just motion for her to continue. “</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T75">Ares got it in his head to create a battle world based on video games. A world with a global magic construct to aid and encourage people to grow stronger. The System has done its job and created a stagnate society.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P36"><text:tab/>“Wait, won’t this piss off Ares?”</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P36"><text:tab/>“This is his fifth or sixth attempt and he’s already moved on. Even got Athena roped into it after figuring out that a world with literally nothing but combat was a bad idea. At least this is the only one that got into the habit of stealing souls from the underworld.”</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P35"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T252">How much do they use that summoning ritual? And why?”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P74"><text:span text:style-name="T72"><text:tab/>“</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T76">There are probably more than a thousand towns and cities in the Empire and each has their own hero squad or two. They get replaced every few years and time flows faster there so its in the millions by now.” Hekate grunts, “As if people wouldn’t abuse a ritual like that as soon as possible. The intent was to clear out strong dungeons cities were exploiting to rule the world. The ritual was supposed to be sanctioned by Ares and paired with a blessing. Now that he abandoned the world it’s used to suppress the growth of every village.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P74"><text:span text:style-name="T70"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T76">The grimace on my face grows as the explanation continues until a question pops into my head. “What do you mean they get replaced every few years?”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P37"><text:tab/>“They’re sent home before they get enough power to be a threat. It’s sold as returning to Earth after their duty is done. The reality is they came from the Styx and they are returned to the Styx.”</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P37"><text:soft-page-break/><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T253">That’s horrible!” I say but Hekate just shrugs. “Alright, you want me to shake things up there. I want to end this hero business. Do you have any suggestions? Any interesting blind spots or lynch pins to target?”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P37"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T253">The System is the heart of everything but it is too well made to bother attacking directly. Each part of it is independent and has no means to remove the power it grants. At first I intended for you to explore the rune system the Empire uses for spells. Dungeons are a better option now. You could simply sneak in and destroy key ones but I bet you can come up with something more creative. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T254">They are pocket dimensions that keep themselves fully stocked with monsters.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T253">”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P74"><text:span text:style-name="T76"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T77">The conversation drifts from there and I decide to take the time to </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T78">fix up my system. A week of not so diligent work and the only solution I have is ripping out everything that causes a problem. It is a good thing there is no rush as the System is solid and heals itself. All of my attributes show as errors now but it can handle shape shifting so it’s a fair trade.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:h text:style-name="P86" text:outline-level="1">Chapter 6</text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="P75"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T79">The crisp night air flows through my snowy fur </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T82">now that I’m back in Prahoria</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T79">. Seven tails wave behind me in enjoyment. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T80">A</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T79"> human form is useful but </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T80">fox form will always be my favorite. The only concession now is that I am the size of a typical fox instead of my normal horse size. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T81">Other than the soot stains on my paws that is. They are still there from my time in hell despite this being a new body.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P75"><text:span text:style-name="T81"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T82">Since subtlety did not work during my escape it is time to flood the field with illusions. That was my go to tactic in Hell since Demons could sense my illusions. Hide trees in illusions of themselves. Make patches of dirt looks a shade darker. Duplicate some patches of leaves. These are the simplest illusions I can make and take more effort to dispel than to create. By morning a sizable patch of the forest is littered with </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T83">decoy illusions.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P75"><text:span text:style-name="T83"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T84">I settle down in the boughs of a tree with a view of Edgewood and wait. There is no way I am going into a pit of monsters without seeing how effective my illusions are on people. Not that I know where a dungeon is in the first place.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P75"><text:span text:style-name="T84"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T85">Eventually a group of people starts gathering just outside the gates of Edgewood. There are about two dozen people in a variety of leather armors milling around. The only constant between them are the empty sacks on their backs. A second group of five stands at the head carrying visible weapons. Between the similar colors on their armor and the</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T86">ir</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T85"> </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T86">attention to the surroundings these five must be the guards for the group.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P75"><text:span text:style-name="T81"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T87">The group starts moving only to stop inches into the tree line. The guards advance on one of the discolored patches of dirt and impale it with a sword. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T88">The illusion does not react in any way even when they start trying to dig it up. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T89">These illusions are designed to be robust and when they try dispelling it only a small patch gets destroyed. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T90">Learning to make illusions break like that took ages in the past but it is so worth it. Just the puckered grimace on their faces makes it worth it.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P39"><text:tab/>The next move of the guards is what I was afraid of and the reason I am watching from as far away as possible. They set out to poke and prod every illusion they can find in the area. That includes the ones that have no effect at all. <text:span text:style-name="T255">Those are as undetectable as I can make illusions so any hope of making illusions that won’t be noticed is out.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P75"><text:span text:style-name="T90"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T91">It would be nice if I could hear what they were saying. I can only assume that everyone here can track my illusions as casually Randel tracked me. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T92">It’s that extra bit I have to add to make the System play nice with my magic causing the problem I bet. They might even be using the same sense as Randel and my illusions just seem like hidden creatures to them. That </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T93">could be interesting</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T92">.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P75"><text:span text:style-name="T92"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T93">The larger group is starting to fidget and look nervous. For some reason the looks are aimed at their guards more than the surroundings. That works for me so I send a wisp of blue foxfire to hang above them. There are around 20 wisps by the time the group decides to start moving again. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T94">Moving all the flames is tough with the distance but doable since they are going the same direction. I follow along once they get into the forest fully.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P75"><text:soft-page-break/><text:span text:style-name="T94"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T95">The guards are on a constant look out so of course the first to notice my foxfire is one of the others that happens to glance upward. It does not take long before everyone except the guards have seen them and no one says anything. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T96">When they reach the blandest stone building I have ever seen in the middle of the forest a couple more guards rush out to stop the group. I dispel my foxfire before any of the original guards can see and watch the argument devolve.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P75"><text:span text:style-name="T96"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T97">I’m pretty sure my goal is inside Fort Ugly but that will take some work to get into safely. They can argue while I head back down the game trail and start enchanting every tree I can around the town. Their senses weren’t able to sort out my foxfire from the crowd so hopeful trees mimicking my System’s presence will mask me. That and adaptive camouflage.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P75"><text:span text:style-name="T97"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T98">A few dozen trees is probably enough to hide me. It is certainly enough to bore me. Camping in the boughs above the trail let me hear anything said so now I just need something to get them talking. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T99">A mauve cloaked figure appears off the trail but close enough to be seen. Only shadows fill the cloth since creating a convincing human is not worth it without relying on mental illusions. I’m not confident in a battle of wills with System augmented people.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P75"><text:span text:style-name="T99"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T100">The same group from Fort Ugly returns and stop as soon as the see my puppet. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T103">The ones with backpacks are now fully laden with dungeon goods I assume.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T100"> The guards approach most fanning out while one takes the lead and demands, “Who are you? What are you doing out here?”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P40"><text:tab/>“Oh? A bit gruff aren’t you?” I say through the illusion with a masculine voice, “I’m just an illusionist practicing my craft.”</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P75"><text:span text:style-name="T100"><text:tab/>“</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T101">So you are the one causing the delays.” glares the guard as his colleagues circle around my puppet, “First Randel disappears with one of the heroes and now you show up to sabotage the town.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P41"><text:tab/>“If you choose to stop for harmless illusions, that is your own decision. Sounds like poor management if that’s all it takes to ‘sabotage the town.’”</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P75"><text:span text:style-name="T101"><text:tab/>“We’ll see what Lord </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T102">Ferridge </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T103">has to say about that.” the guard says and advances.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P42"><text:tab/>“Maybe someday.” I reply before cutting my connection to the illusion. None of them notice the difference until the guard talking to me attempts to grab my puppet only for his hand to pass through without resistance. A few more confused swipes precede his cursing.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P42"><text:tab/>“Sarge, should we go after him?” asks one of the guards.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P42"><text:tab/>“Go where?” scoffs Sarge, “All of the damn trees have a presence and I didn’t see which direction he ran off to. Did you?”</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P42"><text:tab/>They do a cursory search before leaving in frustration. Their wards even flinch at the tone when the group gets moving again. What an interesting fault line. These porters are crucial to the town but not respected. Apparently the dungeon is important enough to build a fort around but not to build a road. <text:span text:style-name="T256">Well, I can contemplate the significance with my eyes closed for now.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P75"><text:soft-page-break/><text:span text:style-name="T103"><text:tab/>“</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T104">Hmm, that fool actually left his construct.” mumbles someone on the ground below. Opening my eyes reveals a mage poking around the puppet I left behind. Glowing runes appear as he fiddles around with a smirk. The smirk disappears when he curses, “</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T105">Right, of course all of the trees emit the same signature</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T104">.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P75"><text:span text:style-name="T104"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T105">He casts something else at my puppet and frowns. His next spell has some area of effect that bursts my illusion and washes over me on the next tree over. My muscles tense as I watch him but he is still focused on the tree in front of him. When he pulls a piece of bark off to look at he grows furious before tossing it on the ground. “Are you serious!? A mass dispel didn’t even get through the bark!?” he stomps off.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P43"><text:tab/>That’s odd. The illusion I used on the trees is hard to remove but not that hard. It should also be attached to the location, not the tree. There must be some System weirdness but it gives me a thought. What happens when something eats part of the tree?</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P38"><text:tab/></text:p>
   <text:h text:style-name="P87" text:outline-level="1">Chapter 7</text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="P65"><text:span text:style-name="T1"><text:tab/>“</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T3">You gained the ability [Sense Presence]!”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P65"><text:span text:style-name="T6"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T106">I found the past week relaxing. The original 5 man patrols searching the forest reduced to just a few pairs of guards. I managed to use illusions to expand my sense of smell to presences. Making sense of a completely new scent was strange. That led to the discovery of a squirrel with my presence. I still ate the </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T107">morsel</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T106">, but </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T107">it</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T106"> </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T107">confirms that my enchantments can spread</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T106">. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T107">With how much mana the System is throwing around that is not too surprising.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P65"><text:span text:style-name="T107"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T108">Prodding the defenses was less successful. Throwing an enchanted pebble at the entrance of the forest fort had a response in seconds. In a minute guards were swarming the surrounding area like ants. That is going to be a tough nut to crack but the town is much more lax. It took hours before anyone even noticed.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P65"><text:span text:style-name="T108"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T109">Today is the time to step </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T110">things</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T109"> up. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T110">The illusions so far make a good </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T111">enough foundation to start another layer. For that I need to decide how to handle things and test how effective mental illusions are. The two scouts in front of me can help with both of those tasks. I form the illusion equivalent of a goofy stick figure and add a mental illusion to make it seem like my previous puppet.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P65"><text:span text:style-name="T111"><text:tab/>“</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T112">Are you looking for anything in particular?” my puppet asks the two scouts.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P44"><text:tab/>“Shit!” shouts one of them, “Don’t suppose this is the real you?”</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P44"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T257">No, just a mouth piece.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P65"><text:span text:style-name="T112"><text:tab/>“</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T113">Not surprising,” he replies but still walks up to poke my puppet, “You’re causing a lot of trouble for the town, you know that?”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P76"><text:span text:style-name="T113"><text:tab/>“</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T114">And it seems the town is causing trouble for me as well, unless you’re just here for a social visit.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P47"><text:tab/>“There!” shouts the second scout as he points at my hiding spot. </text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P77"><text:span text:style-name="T114"><text:tab/>The first </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T115">scout draws his sword and slashes</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T114"> before my mind can switch gears. “</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T115">Ha, as if you could hide that easily! This is for threatening Edgewood!” he taunts as the blades cuts my flesh. Luckily I am much smaller than he expects and I flee with a gash on my back. “A fox? Damn tricky bastard, using a patsy.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P45"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T258">The chase is not easy but my agility is high enough to manage. That and the scouts aren’t very determined to catch me. Either way I can rest and lick my wounds in the safety of liminal space. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T259">Without the chase distracting me I realize that they let me get away. They probably wanted me to lead them to my base. We have grossly misjudged each other.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P46"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T260">Well, I know how to handle things now. Aggressively, but not violently. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T261">These are not men of reason but I don’t like my odds in a straight fight. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T263">If they are driven to attack because of simple illusions then illusions must be a threat to them.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P46"><text:soft-page-break/><text:span text:style-name="T262"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T263">Once you throw out the idea of making mental illusions subtle and undetectable the possibilities get strange. They create sensations directly in the brain but the further it is from raw sense the harder it is. Making people see a flower where there is easily disrupted but seeing the wrong color is much harder to resist. Even if that color is physically impossible and makes no sense.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P78"><text:span text:style-name="T113"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T116">Several hours after night falls I begin setting up floating every color lights </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T117">within view of</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T116"> the fort. They appear as every possible color at once without combining into white and are still a quick way to get a headache if I look at them. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T117">The surrounding trees are all enchanted and we are pointed away from anything important. It is time to send the message. A deep groaning rumble builds into a full minute long roar loud enough to rattle the entire garrison.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P78"><text:span text:style-name="T117"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T118">The first </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T119">guards charge out only to fall to the ground clutching their eyes and shouting in pain. The pile up grows until a vaguely familiar looking man in robes comes out. He falters some after seeing the illusions but does not need to avert his eyes. Instead he grows dour and advances to stand in front of the fallen guards. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T120">A ring of runes appear in the air around the hand he raises before a ball of fire shoots out.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P78"><text:span text:style-name="T120"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T4">“</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T5">You gained the ability [Fire Resistance]!”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P48"><text:tab/>That ball is too powerful and I am too close. A wave of fire washes over the patch of forest engulfing me as well after it impacts the ground. The underbrush and all of my illusions are erased but fire is not much of a threat to me personally. My ability with foxfire does not extend to full pyrokinesis but I can at least control other flames enough to avoid burns. Well, maybe not a direct hit with something like that. It is passed time to leave.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P48"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T264">That is the second time I got hit </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T265">but at least they are in no shape to chase this time</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T264">. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T265">It is time for a break. A few days in NYC will be a nice palate cleanser then I can come back with fresh eyes. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T266">First a stop at Hekate’s place.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P48"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T267">Made a fine mess there.” Hekate laughs over tea, “I am surprised you decided to plaster your name over the entire thing.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P48"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T268">Wait, what do you mean</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T267">?” I blink in confusion at that last part.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P48"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T268">You taught that forest how to get around the world’s System block on outside magic. That knowledge is spreading like wildfire and it involves copying the signature of your System. Even if they don’t know you personally everyone is going to be aware of your presence before long.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P78"><text:span text:style-name="T121"><text:tab/>“</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T122">Oh.” I lean back in my seat. The spread was obvious but I never considered the extent. Or the rate for that matter. That is going to alarm people. “</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T123">Yeah, that’s going to attract more attention than I want, but it’s not like my plan to just blend into the background was working.” I lament, “</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T193">I’m going to </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T194">take a vacation and come back with fresh eyes</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T193">.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P78"><text:span text:style-name="T193"><text:tab/>“</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T195">Oh,” Hekate chuckles, “That’s not a bad idea but you might to sort out your system first. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T196">Without the physical augments it won’t draw enough ambient mana to cause serious problems. It will still stand out more if left as it is.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T195">”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P78"><text:soft-page-break/><text:span text:style-name="T195"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T196">That is a good point. Earth doesn’t have near the mana levels of Prahoria and drawing too deeply on it is a good way to make enemies. I have no idea what people will make of the mangled mess of an enchantment </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T197">that is my System</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T196">. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T197">This is going to take a while and some help. Hekate rolls her eyes at my pleading look</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T196"> </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T197">before agreeing.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P64"/>
   <text:h text:style-name="P88" text:outline-level="1">Chapter 8</text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="P79"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T124">Despite the odd feeling of a System properly grafted to my soul, the week long vacation in New York was lovely and just what I needed to put things together in a new way. It’s not exactly a new idea but this is the first time I found a use for an illusory avatar. Creating an illusion of myself and using paired ones to let me see through its eyes would be too limited for use. Access to liminal space changes that by giving me a safe space to puppet it from.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P79"><text:span text:style-name="T124"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T125">Naturally, the complications start immediately. Prahoria is not where I left it. After a moment to … process the idea of losing an entire world, I begin the weird process of trying to find something in a liminal space. Walking around while trying to conceptually blur my idea of the forest is mindbending, but that’s what it takes to navigate The Woods. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T126">It doesn’t take long to find out the missing forest. It grew. In just a week it managed to grow into a light jungle. I guess the System is throwing around way more mana than I thought if this </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T127">is </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T126">the result of weakening its control a little.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P79"><text:span text:style-name="T126"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T128">Stepping into prahoria with my modified System is a rush of new smells. It’s not just the plant life. The System is no longer impeding my magical senses and being born a mundane fox instead of a full spirit </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T129">those senses</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T128"> piggyback on my strongest sense. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T129">I don’t know that I will ever sort out all of the new information but the most common one smells familiar. Like </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T130">a </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T129">home.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P79"><text:span text:style-name="T129"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T131">After a break to listen to the leaves rustle and let the breeze flow through my fur I put my plan into action. Creating an illusory copy of myself and connecting my senses to it is easy. Stretching the connection into the Woods is doable. Piloting two bodies is an absolute mess. I don’t want to know what it would be like if they weren’t synchronized but I still have to deal with two slightly different perspectives. Closing my eyes doesn’t work since I end up wandering out of the Woods </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T132">and colliding with my illusion. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T133">Ah well, it’s good enough I guess.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P49"><text:tab/>Toddling up to the forest fortress I get stopped by a massive cliff. I knew just walking into the fortress was a long shot but I didn’t even know it was possible to create an endless wall of stone in the middle of The Woods. <text:span text:style-name="T269">Even though the area in Prahoria is clear my illusion can’t advance without disconnecting and an arrow just flew through my head.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P79"><text:span text:style-name="T133"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T134">I jump to the side and stare at the projectile lodged in the ground in a stunning display of reflexes. “This is why I avoid straight fights.” I say as more arrows fly through me. Unlike before the top of the fortress is manned by several archers fully willing to waste their ammo. The rate of fire dies down before the fortress gates grind open and out </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T135">mar</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T134">ches the same wizard from before.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P79"><text:span text:style-name="T134"><text:tab/>“</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T135">Hello,” I get out before the lightning starts flying. Five minutes I spend flopping around trying to dodge the bolts shooting from the mans hand before he gets tired. Every hit leaves behind a hole punched clean through my illusion and it’s quite the sight. I never really consider then implication of creating a full copy of an animal before but there are lots of bits never meant for the light of day now visible. No bleeding of course so I just look like some kind of horrible zombie fox.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P79"><text:span text:style-name="T135"><text:tab/>“</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T136">What manner of abomination are you?” mused the man.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P79"><text:soft-page-break/><text:span text:style-name="T136"><text:tab/>“It’s just insult after insult with you.” I reply, “I am a fox, thank you and I’</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T137">m here about the fortress</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T136">. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T137">It’s quite an interesting setup you have here.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T136">”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P79"><text:span text:style-name="T136"><text:tab/>“</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T138">There is nothing for you here!” he says, “If you continue spreading your corruption the Empire will see to your death!”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P79"><text:span text:style-name="T138"><text:tab/>“Oh? Makes me think there is something for me here, but what corruption could a </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T139">humble fox </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T138">like me possibly spread?”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P79"><text:span text:style-name="T138"><text:tab/>He glares, “You have the same mana as the foul magics writhing through the forest </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T140">opposing the Divine System</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T138">.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P79"><text:span text:style-name="T138"><text:tab/>“</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T141">Well shit, this is going to turn into some weird holy war thing isn’t it.” I sigh, “Is there no middle ground? The System is nice and all but why does everything have to follow it?”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P79"><text:span text:style-name="T141"><text:tab/>“</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T142">Heresy!”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P79"><text:span text:style-name="T142"><text:tab/>“Ah, I see. You’ve become too dependent on it.” That’s what I expected after hearing that these people call themselves ‘The Empire’ but it’s still a disappointing confirmation. They’re drowning in fear that their precious System might break despite it being the most resilient magical working I have ever seen. “</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T144">There </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T145">never a chance for compromise, huh?</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T143">”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P79"><text:span text:style-name="T143"><text:tab/>“</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T145">What a foolish thought!” he laughs and I ditch my illusory avatar. Might as well let him talk at it while I do something else.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P79"><text:span text:style-name="T145"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T146">After preparing another illus</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T147">o</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T146">ry copy of myself I shrink down to size of a normal fox and apply some camouflage. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T148">It’s time to get serious. Popping back into Prahoria behind the fortress I place down the prepared illusion out of the way. When my usual illusions aren’t stubborn enough I have another enchantment that can link multiple copies together and let them repair each other. By nightfall there are over a dozen glaring foxes sitting near the fortress. On a whim I add a hiss to one of them that soon propagates to all of them. That should be good cover for sneaking into the fortress.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P79"><text:span text:style-name="T148"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T149">Before that I want a better look at the fortresses wards. The enchanted plants that have grown near the walls are likely enough cover but archers are now distracted firing at the decoy foxes too. With my System fully integrated I can now smell the magical signature. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T150">That means I can copy it and mask my own. At some point during my practice the defenders switch to fireballs and colorful curses loud enough to hear from the other side of the fortress. Time to give them something else to focus on.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P50"><text:tab/>Normally the wards woven into the walls would prevent any further enchantment. My illusions are so fine that they act like a fluid and can seep into typical wards just fine. That means drawing a giant crude cock over the front the fortress is trivial for me. I even use an impossible color to make it a proper eldritch eyesore.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P50"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T270">The gate opens as soon as I scurry away letting me know I succeeded in triggering alarms. There is still someone keeping an eye on my effigies but it still isn’t hard to sneak up a tree and onto the second floor. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T271">Any sounds from the door I slip through are masked by the ambient hissing. The </text:span><text:soft-page-break/><text:span text:style-name="T271">building isn’t large enough to get lost in. A barracks, an atrium, a portal chamber and I’m on the other side within seconds.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P79"><text:span text:style-name="T150"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T151">The term Dungeon did nothing to prepare me for the scene I find. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T152">The crystalline arch behind me dumps me in the middle of a full encampment. It is a good thing night is well underway in here too. There is a strange mix of large tents and bland stone buildings surrounded by a sandstone wall. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T153">A sandstone wall with the same scent of enchantments as the fortress. That I can blend in with and take a break.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P80"><text:span text:style-name="T153"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T154">A few patrols have passed by my spot both in the town and on the wall above. It’s been a few hours and there is no alarm or even a sense of urgency. Sneaking into the Dungeon is a success as soon as I hop over this wall.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P80"><text:span text:style-name="T154"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T155">In a mirror of the other side of the portal the forest starts right outside the walls. It’s a familiar forest even if I’ve never been to this section. One filled with conjured trees that can’t be enchanted. I don’t want to check but grit my teeth and walk out of the the Woods back into Prahoria right outside the mini fort. This entire time I could just walk right into the Dungeon.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P51"><text:tab/>It’s a win. I’ve accomplished my goal. I have a secure route into and out of the dungeon. Now I am going to go take a victory nap. Hopefully when I wake up it will actually feel like a victory.</text:p>
   <text:h text:style-name="P89" text:outline-level="1">Chapter 9</text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="P80"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T156">Since soldiers were still combing the forest outside the Dungeon I made sure to give them things to find. Some distorted images of the forest at head height, light filters to make the forest more purple and spooky, and flowers with impossible colors for example. The tripwires that make suspicious noises are my favorite though.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P52"><text:tab/>Most of my time the past few days were spent exploring the Dungeon. <text:span text:style-name="T272">I was expecting something less mundane but the Dungeon is just a slightly odd forest. There are bears, wolves and pigs wandering around in numbers far too high for the space. The only points of interest are a few watering holes and the odd cave complete with resident bear. Oh and the endless cliffs wrapping around the whole thing. A complete disappointment at first since it just looked like a poor imagining of a forest. However that realization let things click into place.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P52"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T272">The Dungeon is the imagining of the System. This is a support pillar for the world decorated like a forest because it has to attach to the forest outside. It looks like it supports the economy and the military as well. There should be some kind of anchor around here to make sure nothing slips out of place. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T273">Now where could it be? </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T274">For that matter what does it look like?</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P80"><text:span text:style-name="T156"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T157">This place covers at least a few square miles and so far I’ve only glanced around. The </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T158">non-monstrous </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T157">monsters are as good a place as any to start since they will be in the way no matter what. The pigs are the safest choice but I want the familiarity of another canine. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T158">Finding a pack of a half dozen wolves doesn’t take long and they aren’t immediately hostile when I reveal myself either.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P80"><text:span text:style-name="T158"><text:tab/>Being half again their size put me in the category of tough but doable fight so they were wary but calm. As they began to rise I crouched at the edge of the clearing </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T159">to project friendliness. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T160">We danced around each other until one of the wolves got brave enough to approach and let me get a feel for their magical signature. Changing my signature to match is like flipping a switch. All of a sudden tails started wagging and I am swarmed by curious canines. After the customary sniffing we all settled down for some mutual grooming. The honest simplicity of wild animals is refreshing.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P80"><text:span text:style-name="T160"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T161">While relaxing with the pack I feel a shift in the wind. Not the physical wind but the mana currents. The wolves actually leak a lot more mana than Earth creatures would. There’s enough leakage than I can feel when it starts drifting toward something nearby. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T162">I can’t really see anything from just an idle glance and it disappears before I can decide to investigate. Just a wild guess, but that was probably a person sneaking around. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T163">Running would be smart but I think I want to see this fight.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P80"><text:span text:style-name="T162"><text:tab/>Soon enough the mana shifts again only to </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T163">pick up speed as the rest of the group arrives. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T164">The wolves follow suit when I stand and start growling. We don’t wait long before our enemies rushed in. I however am distracted by the arrow that pierces clean through my heart. I can hear the fighting around me but the blood pour onto the ground is all I can see as I collapse. This is the most pain I have felt in a long time. Fortunately, a simple hole is a solvable problem for a shapeshifter.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P80"><text:soft-page-break/><text:span text:style-name="T164"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T165">The healing is almost automatic in my true form so the illusion laced roar does not escape my notice. My attention snapped to the heavily armored man in front as all of the wolves began piling on themselves to attack him. We are not having that. My own illusion laced voice rings out to counter it. If he wants my allies attention then I will make him seem like the most boring thing around. The wolve</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T166">s pick new targets and I can see the panic in his eyes.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P80"><text:span text:style-name="T166"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T167">There are four of them. The armored vanguard is leading of course but behind him are a couple of people in leather armor. One with a staff and glowing runes floating around. She looks ready to run. The other is pissed and wielding an enormous ax with a couple dead wolves at his feet already. The final menace is up a tree lining up another shot with his bow. Soon another wolf lies dead with an arrow to the heart.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P80"><text:span text:style-name="T167"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T168">Many powerful kitsune can create fire hot enough to vaporize a man. I am not one of them though. I prefer versatility and find napalm fascinating. Goo fire is such a neat concept and a pair of balls fly from my tails to the enemy front liners. Both are blocked </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T169">but</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T168"> </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T169">that just leaves them holding a burning ax and shield. The shield user doesn’t even flinch but the ax wielder drops his weapon and leaves an opening for one of the two wolves attacking him. The last is dealing with the magic woman so that just leaves the archer. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T170">The archer that just shot me with another arrow.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P80"><text:span text:style-name="T170"><text:tab/>A much larger ball of foxfire flies toward the archer. It’s slow enough for him to jump to another branch but the ball just follows. That’s fine since he can’t shoot anyone while running away. After a marry chase the flames catch him. Somehow he is not burning but </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T173">is </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T170">still tr</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T173">ying frantically</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T170"> to put out the fire. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T171">Some kind of System shenanigans I guess but I can feed those flames for a long time. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T172">It’s not needed though as some kind of threshold is reached and the flailing man turns into a burning corpse with no warning. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T173">I think this is the first time some one died without even trying to break my concentration.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P53"><text:tab/>“Oh, they’re all dead.” I say turning my attention back to the sole remaining fighter. The armored man stood waiting for my next move. However, all of my wolf friends lay dead among the bodies of his teammates. <text:span text:style-name="T275">It’s not a surprise but the tears come anyway.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P53"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T275">You can talk!” he says before pulling out a flare and launching it into the sky, “You monster, you killed my friends! Now you will be hunted down!”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P53"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T275">As did you, fellow monster. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T276">What was even the point of this fight?</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T275">”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P53"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T276">Variants are threat to humanity! </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T277">You are to be killed on sight and I can see why now!”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P80"><text:span text:style-name="T173"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T174">I blink at him as the stupidity filters through my mind, “An Empire engaged in a preventive war. I should have known. The most insidious form of cowardice. We’re done here.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P80"><text:span text:style-name="T174"><text:tab/>At least he’s smart enough to not do more than complain as I leave. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T175">Once back in the The Woods I flop on the ground to brood. How ironic that the so called monsters are friendlier than any of the humans here. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T176">Although it was nice of that man to inform me of variants. If dungeons can mutate like that the Empires fear of them makes more sense. All the more reason to find that core.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P80"><text:soft-page-break/><text:span text:style-name="T176"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T177">The next day I set out with the new knowledge. An illusory shroud to show I’m not a threat to the monsters and an eye to the mana currents to avoid humans is all I need to wander with impunity. Ironically, the patrols looking for me have a much larger affect on the ambient mana. Their strength makes them easy to avoid.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P80"><text:span text:style-name="T177"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T178">The bears and pigs are no where near as friendly as the wolves but they are the only things I manage to find. That is until I realize that the strong mana pull I have been avoiding near the edge has been in the same place for days now. They are guarding something which means I want to know what it is. After swapping to my neutral shroud I set out to poke my nose where it probably is not wanted.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P80"><text:span text:style-name="T178"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T179">Sure enough embedded in the cliff face is a massive gem. Glowing veins radiate from it </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T180">pulsing with power. The complication is the group of five standing in front of it. That head mage and the other four heroes summoned with me. Now how should I handle this reunion?</text:span></text:p>
   <text:h text:style-name="P90" text:outline-level="1">Chapter 10</text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="P81"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T181">It would be nice to use the same illusion trick to talk to them, but the dungeon is already in a liminal space. Trying to maintain the spell from Prahoria instead is a bad idea for a variety of reasons. I guess I could try hiding but these trees aren’t going to offer much protection. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T182">Ah, the wall. I can hide behind the outer wall of the dungeon. Probably. Maybe.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P81"><text:span text:style-name="T182"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T183">The answer is yes, the wall is thin enough to control an illusion from the other side. Thin may not be the right word as the depth is mutable just like everything else in The Woods. I’m actually a little worried about poking a hole. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T184">My plans change when I come within sight of the core and realize I can see it on both sides of the wall. Parking my illusion behind a bush I head up to my prize without bothering about the guests.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P81"><text:span text:style-name="T184"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T185">I get a delightful surprise when I walk up and touch the massive gem. Divine constructs aren’t built like machines, they are grown as some kind of quasi-life. That means they can be negotiated with if you can ‘speak’ their language. I assumed that would be a long drawn out process, but the system embedded in me reacts immediately. A visual prompt appears asking if I want to reset the dungeon but I can also feel the communications beneath that facade. I guess this is why the Empire needs to summon people.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P54"><text:tab/>Dismissing the prompt earns me a peculiar sense of relief. <text:span text:style-name="T278">That is as good a starting point as anything for my conversation with the Dungeon core. It has two purposes. Maintain the separation between Prahoria and the rest of the Underworld and challenge any humans that enter. The reset feature is effectively a signal that the Dungeon is out of order but the Empire simply uses as a periodic reset mechanism. It is not happy to learn that all its frustration was pointless since there were never any failures to correct in the first place.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P81"><text:span text:style-name="T185"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T186">From there I share whatever ideas I can think of that might be interesting to the rock. Alternate ways to challenge people besides pure combat are well received. Changing the entrance </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T187">is a big no</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T186">. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T187">The buildings built inside the dungeon are a different matter. Dungeons have some kind of self cleaning feature but the buildings refuse to be cleaned. The core is skeptical of using force to remove them until I explain that they are an odd form of armor rather than an odd form of human droppings. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T188">The Empire should appreciate that.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P81"><text:span text:style-name="T188"><text:tab/>“</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T189">I think I see something.” said someone reminding me that I still had an active avatar.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P58"><text:tab/>“Oh hey Alex.” I say after reattaching the avatar and seeing the woman how pointed me out.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P81"><text:span text:style-name="T189"><text:tab/>“</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T190">What-” She recoils as I rise from my hiding spot, “Why do you sound like Kim, monster?”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P58"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T279">That’s rude. I am Kim the beautiful vixen! Definitely not monstrous </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T280">here.” I preen, “I can’t hold a candle to the brutes of this world.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P55"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T280">What happened? You abandoned us and now you turned into a monster?”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P55"><text:soft-page-break/><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T280">I’ve always been a fox and you could have come with me. You can still come with me. I don’t mind bringing you back to New York, but you would have to find your way from there.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P55"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T281">She will not be going anywhere since she has to pay off your debts.” says the head mage, “The consequences of your actions naturally fall on your associates.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P55"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T281">Oh cut the crap. You are the one at fault for summoning me.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P55"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T281">Make whatever excuses you want, but as long as you keep up these games your friends here will suffer for it.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P55"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T281">I slump at the truth of his words. Alex and </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T288">Arthur</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T281"> look pissed but Noah looks worried. What a rotten situation, but I can’t just leave them. “Alright, if you let them leave with me I will never return to this world again.” </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T282">It’s an honest offer, </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T283">probably </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T282">the best he will get. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T283">Somehow I doubt he will go for it.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P55"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T283">Who says we want to go with you?” Alex shouts </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T284">and that stings more than I expected. At least Noah looks hopeful.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P55"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T285">Your debts will be paid.” the mage smirks, “Either you can cease this childish rebellion, surrender and work them off or they will fall to your friends. Every problem you cause will cost them dearly.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P55"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T285">I scoff, “There are no debts. You are just a sadistic monster and surrender would only make things worse.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P56"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T286">So you’re just going to turn down his offer? You think there will be a better one later or something? Or do you just want us to pay for your mistakes?” </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T288">Arthur</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T286"> speaks up, “You really are a monster, aren’t you?”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P56"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T286">I swallow my first reply before it can cause problems and ask, “ </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T288">Arthur</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T286"> where do you think that offer leads?”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P55"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T286">Just answer the question.” Alex whispers.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P55"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T286">I can see the resolve in the woman’s eyes and as much as I want to explain things there is no point. Even if it is a false hope I am still the one to crush it and she is already preparing to hate me for it. “No, I will not take the offer. I am willing to help you escape but not by walking into a</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T287">n obvious</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T286"> trap.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P56"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T287">Oh, you think you are too good for the rules?” </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T288">Arthur</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T287"> mocks. Alex appears vindicated while the mage’s mood is dropping. I make a point not to look at Noah though. He actually appears to be considering my words and I don’t want to draw attention to him.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P55"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T287">Pretty much. This isn’t my country, so why should I care about it’s rules?” I shrugged, “Anyway, if you want out later come find me.” I would be shocked if he did but stranger things have happened.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P55"><text:soft-page-break/><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T288">Nobody is going anywhere.” declares the mage, “Do you really think you can escape the Empire’s might?” He launches into a tirade but I have other things to do.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P55"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T288">I need a distraction maybe multiple of them. Something that will absorb the mage’s attention. My tampering with the dungeon would work if he knew about it but just telling him won’t. I can’t cast an illusion on the core itself and asking for permission doesn’t work either. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T289">Not surprising. It is surprising when asking the dungeon to do it itself works. I guess magical signatures are just not important to gemstones.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P55"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T290">Hopping into Prahoria I prepare to enter the dungeon near my avatar. After applying my best cloaking illusions, I also adjust my avatar to look at the core and wait. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T291">It doesn’t take long before they are fussing over the dungeon and I pop in behind my avatar.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P57"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T291">If you want out now is the time.” I whisper to Noah with an illusion only he can hear. Fortunately he is at the back of the group when he turns. “You wanna come with?”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P57"><text:span text:style-name="T291"><text:tab/>He only checks the others once before sneaking away. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T292">The mage notices before he can reach me and blows my front leg off with bolt of lightning. Noah rushes over as I cry out from the pain. That bastard mage just ignores my glare as he saunters over. Well I’m not out of tricks and belch out a cloud of smoke to cover our retreat. Getting that to work with physical illusions was way too finicky but has been invaluable.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P57"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T292">The smoke isn’t </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T293">just </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T292">a distraction it’s to break line of sight so I can get Noah and myself out without bringing stowaways. Arthur is apparently the type of person that charges into situation completely blind and follow</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T293">s</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T292"> us to the outside. “</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T294">Did you want to leave too?” </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T295">I ask.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P57"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T295">I didn’t expect you to shirk your duty for a monster, Noah.” Arthur glares at the man while ignoring me.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P57"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T295">Duty to kidnappers,” I snort, “What a joke.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P57"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T296">I just can’t Arthur.” Noah grimaces, “I’m not cut out for monster fights.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P57"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T296">You’re just a coward.” Arthur spits, “We’ve been given the opportunity to get true power and you just want to give that up? As if this isn’t an obvious trap that’s just going to lead to your death.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P57"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T297">I can’t help but laugh, “Is that what you think you’re doing? Getting ‘true power’ just handed to you?”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P57"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T298">Shut up, bitch!” He yells and locks eyes with me. That is enough for me to send him an illusion. The Waking Dream is my most and least powerful mental illusion. Since it feeds the target exactly what they expect to see it’s nigh impossible to detect without knowing what to look for. That are they try to touch something that’s not there. Giving the target complete control of the illusion means it rarely lasts long. It’s about the only illusion I trust to actually work here though. Arthur smirking at some imagined reaction of mine before turning to rant at Noah shows my trust is not misplaced.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P57"><text:soft-page-break/><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T299">Limping around is getting old fast so I pull off my injured leg and toss it to the side. Shuffling things around with shapeshifting is easy but regrowing something that damaged would take too long. It’s quicker to just shift to two legs </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T300">than to heal my injuries</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T299">. Noah </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T300">is </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T299">speechless as we shuffle to a tree away from my decoy leg. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T300">Now we wait under camouflage for Arthur to break the illusion.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P57"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T301">He does not take long and roars in rage once he realizes the trick. Once he spots the dismembered leg he stomps off in a rampage. Noah and I head the other way and make it into The Woods without issue.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P57"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T301">Alright we’re safe.” I declare and sit down, “Let’s rest for a bit. The rest of the journey is just some walking but that escape was way trickier than I expected.” Noah takes a seat as well but it’s clear he’s operating on auto pilot. It’s going to take a bit for things to sink in.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P57"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T302">A rumble of grinding stones reverberates through the ground. The source is the massive wall of the dungeon as it begins moving. This is the first time I have ever seen a cliff face scoot off into the sunset. Yep, it’s going to take a for things to sink in.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:h text:style-name="P92" text:outline-level="1">Chapter 11</text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="P93"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T6">“</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T191">I’m sorry.” Noah says.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P59"><text:tab/>“Hmm. For what?” I say with a head tilt.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P59"><text:tab/>“You’re…” he stammers as he stares at where my other arm should be, “You lost your arm because of me.”</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P93"><text:span text:style-name="T191"><text:tab/>“It’s not a big deal. Not for kitsune at least.” </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T192">At my age I’m more spirit than flesh. Even losing my head isn’t fatal. Complicated yes, but not fatal. “I underestimate that mage guy and paid for it. Still I knew the risks and decided to act anyway. Still apology accepted even if you didn’t need it.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P60"><text:tab/>As he sinks back into his own head I shift into a more human form. It’s much more comfortable than walking around as some weird fox human hybrid. There’s no point in fixing myself either since it will take materials to do that and I don’t feel like cannibalizing my own body.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P60"><text:tab/>“Why?” Noah whispers. When I prompt him to continue he asks, “Why are you being so nice to me?”</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P60"><text:tab/>“Why not? It’s a nice thing to do.”</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P60"><text:tab/>“Nobody takes that kind of risk for no reason. <text:span text:style-name="T306">Especially not when…” he trails off looking at my stump.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P60"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T306">I roll my eyes, “Kindness works best when it’s not transactional. I helped you because it pleased me and nudged the world in a direction I like.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P60"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T306">Oh.” he goes quiet again. “You aren’t going to eat me or something.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P60"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T306">I stare at him as I try to follow that train of thought before giving up. “What? </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T307">Alright, you lost me there.” I pause as the implications catch up, “Wait, did you follow me thinking you might be eaten? What the hell is going on back there?”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P60"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T308">You’ve kind of been causing a lot of problems. You killed Randel, corrupted the forest and destroyed that adventurer grou</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T309">p. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T310">Is it really that big of a leap?</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T308">”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P60"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T310">I guess I can understand even if the archer is the only one I actually hurt, but how bad was it after I left for you to follow me?” I’m just going to assume he was being optimistic and not suicidal.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P60"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T311">It wasn’t too bad at first. They were confused as much as upset until </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T312">you know that whole corruption thing.” He starts poking at the ground with unseeing eyes, “Each failure to deal with the </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T314">forest’s corruption</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T312"> just made things worse. It wasn’t really anything overt but even Merin got more and more hostile. The more time went on the less I liked the idea of being a soldier. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T313">I’m just too weak</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T312">. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T313">Did I make the right choice?</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T312">”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P60"><text:soft-page-break/><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T313">You made your choice and weakness is not a problem, it’s normal.” I say and get up. When I offer my hand he stares at it. “This next part isn’t hard but it is important you don’t lose sight of me. This isn’t a physical place and The Woods will change when you aren’t looking.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P60"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T313">That got Noah to take my hand although now he </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T314">doesn’t want to let go. I guess we can hold hands on the way to Lady Hekate’s garden. At least he won’t get lost that way.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P60"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T315">So what does the forest’s corruption look like from the other side?” I ask after the silence stretches long enough, “I just wanted a few distractions but it seems to have taken on a life of it’s own.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P60"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T315">That’s an understatement.” he responds, “Having every bush and tree register as a person to [Sense Presence] is terrifying. It made the whole place feel haunted like we were just waiting for something to jump out when we headed to the dungeon. Then a damn squirrel showed up and scared the shit of me </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T316">before I realized it wasn’t a crazed </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T318">assassin</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T316"> or something</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T315">. Yeah, I can understand why they tried to burn it down.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P60"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T317">I stifle a giggle but still get a pout. “Ah, what happened when they tried to burn it down?”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P60"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T317">The trees stopped burning.” he shrugs to my shock. “From what I heard they started a forest fire after realizing the spread was too fast. It worked well enough at first but a few trees didn’t burn. Then more survived untouched. Eventually there weren’t enough burning to sustain the fire. Personally, I’m glad that didn’t work.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P60"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T318">That’s pretty terrifying. Trees are way more social and coordinated than people think, but they don’t work that fast.” I say. I guess with all of the chaotic mana shed by the System a forest could evolve that fast but to become fire proof… Did they copy me again? I didn’t know that was possible but where else could a forest learn something like that? </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T319">Well s</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T318">ucks for the Empire </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T319">either way</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T318">.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P60"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T319">It’s not long before we come up to Lady Hekate’s garden. “We can continue straight to Earth, but it would be good idea to take a detour. Leaving your System ‘as is’ will cause problems on Earth but Granny Kate can fix that. Are you OK with visiting her?”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P60"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T320">She’s not a witch or something, right?” Noah asks.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P60"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T320">I laugh, “She’s the goddess of witches, but your name isn’t Hansel so it’s fine.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P61"><text:tab/>“Ah,” he grows paler but only takes a little prodding to get moving. <text:span text:style-name="T321">Lady Hekate is already waiting for us and soon we are inside enjoying the cozy atmosphere </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T322">as we sit at her breakfast table</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T321">. Well, I’m enjoying it at least.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P61"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T322">Well now, what a lovely surprise! Little Kim has brought a boy home!” Hekate cackles as Noah blushes. I just roll my eyes. “Ah, but this is quite the complicated situation to unravel. Might as well get the easy part out of the way. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T323">Noah do you want me to remove that System for you?” she asks and gets a nod after a beat. “Alright, done.” she says with a snap of her fingers, “</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T324">You’re connection to Prahoria is severed.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P61"><text:soft-page-break/><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T324">It’s gone.” Noah whispers as tension leaves his body, “I’m free.” Tears roll down his face as unseen weight lifts from his shoulders. The bittersweet smile of Hekate tells me things are more complicated but she has not intention of ruining his moment.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P61"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T324">Instead she turns to me and says, “You’ve done well but you left a calling card behind.” </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T325">I know what she’s referring to but not why it matters that I left my arm behind. “It wasn’t your arm because that is not your body.” She continues bringing the pieces together in my mind. Even when detached an arm is still magically connected to the body. It’s not a strong connection and any attempt to use it is easily rebuffed. A complete non issue if it was my arm but it’s the arm of a puppet with no capacity to defend itself.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P61"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T325">Well, shit. This is a pickle. I don’t really want to ditch this body.” I say and consider the dilemma. Well no, it’s not really a dilemma. I don’t really care about the body that much so the choice should be obvious. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T326">Maybe hell gave me some masochistic tendencies because I want to walk into this trap. I want to see what they do. I want to test </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T328">this</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T326"> Empire.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P61"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T329">Huh?” Noah says with visible confusion, “Who’s body is it?”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P61"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T329">I supposed that’s as good a lead in as any.” Hekate answers for me, “That summoning ritual the two of you got caught it actually made new bodies for you. Kim was already a spirit so she’s just possessing a blank body. You however are technically a revenant. A well crafted one without the typical problems of undeath but still some complications.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P61"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T329">I’m not too surprised.” Noah shrugs, “I already figured I died.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P61"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T329">That’s where it gets weird. You weren’t resurrected, you were pulled from the River Styx. It’s full of fragments of souls so you are an amalgam. A brand new person with memories stitched together from other people. I strongly recommend you don’t try to follow up on them. There are plenty of other options but that one would be awkward at best.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P61"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T330">Oh, so that’s why I can’t remember anyone’s face. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T329">That sucks.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P61"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T329">It does, but there’s no rush. You’</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T330">re welcome here</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T329">. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T330">Take your time.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T329">”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:h text:style-name="P94" text:outline-level="1">Chapter 12</text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="P61"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T331">Noah is handling his existential crisis well all things considered. He might even end up staying with Lady Hekate. The Empire however has left me waiting for two days now. If I knew it would take this long I might have just fixed my arm. Instead I am still a one armed human since it’s probably better than a three legged fox.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P61"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T332">The summoning comes with only enough warning for me to notice before my System accepts it. Alright, I’m done with this body. The System can have it. Well not yet. I’m back in the same bland summoning room facing the same summoner. This time the only company we have is a pair of armed guards.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P61"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T333">So you’ve decided to double down on your mistake.” I say as the two guards try to cuff me. They did not plan on my missing arm. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T334">Still I can feel the one cuff digging into my System. Loose threads of magic drape over my spirit searching for </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T335">missing pieces.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P61"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T336">We’ll just add the healing to your growing debt.” Tarrick says before casting magic to heal my arm. Instead of a human arm, the same fox limb I lost grew back with a mess of warped flesh at the interface. I am glad I dampened my sense of pain before being summoned.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P61"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T336">You botched it. I want a refund.” I say as the guard secures the other cuff. A little bit of shape shifting solves the problem. I make sure the cuff falls off in the process. The three of them just stare at it as if peering into the abyss.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P61"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T336">Tarricks looks like he’s cracked the code and orders, </text:span>“<text:span text:style-name="T336">Apply the suppressor correctly this time. And you,” He grabs my neck with cold eyes. My body convulses as lightning tears through it. “You will learn respect. Put her in a cell.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P61"><text:tab/>“… <text:span text:style-name="T336">refuse.” I cough out from my undignified heap on the floor as he exits. Even without the pain lightning is not easy to deal with. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T337">It’s not a good sign for them if this is their reaction to reality peeking through their delusions.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P61"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T338">I get dragged into another bland room but this time it has a reinforced door and no inlaid magic circle. It’s also in the basement and colder than would be comfortable for someone without foxfire. Ironically this is the first time I don’t need to worry about the Empire’s agents while poking at the System. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T339">No one else would want to be here with how low the mana levels here are. They’re all the way back down to normal Earth levels. The magic suppression in this room would be torment for anyone used to System augmentation.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P61"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T340">My awareness turns inward as I sit down to meditate. It’s going to take something more than my usual glamours to trick the System. They’re only skin deep and </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T341">wouldn’t fool the System</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T340">. Whatever mechanism is hiding me right now is beyond my abilities but the trees figured something out </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T341">so I can too. It’s not like I’ve got anything better to do anyway.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P61"><text:soft-page-break/><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T351">I sit and begin my meditating on the issue. It’s time to become the illusion! I’ve always sucked at those riddles like ‘what’s the sound on one hand clapping.’ Shapeshifting, illusions and spiritual possession are all within my means though. They just need some combining. Unfortunately the only idea I have is to repeatedly poke myself with an illusion. It’s dumb but I need to get a feel for exactly why its doesn’t work. At least nobody else can see anything.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P61"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T341">Hours must have passed since lunch arrives in the form of a bowl of suspicious stew </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T342">pushed through a flap on the door</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T341">. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T342">It’s not bad for prison food. Not good either. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T343">Tarrick returns not long after I finish eating and lets me out of the cell. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T344">That doesn’t last long as we enter another cell with a table and chair instead of the mana suppression.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P61"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T344">Sit,” Tarrick orders and I obey after a moment of consideration. I already know what he does against resistance. What will he do when he finds none? “Now it’s quite clear that you’ve caused a lot of trouble for Edgewood. First </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T348">murdering your training officer, then</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T344"> corrupting the forest the town relies on and now kidnapping one of our heroes. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T345">Those are serious offenses. Is there anything you would like to say?”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P61"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T345">They aren’t true but it sounds like you already made up your mind.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P61"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T345">Look I understand you come from a different world but you are in the Empire now. Things may seem unfair but laws have to be followed. They are the foundation of society. Without them we would just have chaos. In your own words, what were you doing?”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P62"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T345">Seeing Tarrick being non confrontational is so weird I have to take a second. Right, I forgot how different things must look from his perspective. He thinks he surprised me with that summoning and put me in a torment cell. Damn this manipulation probably works well on most people. It’</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T347">s kind of working here.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P63"><text:span text:style-name="T345"><text:tab/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T347">There’s no point in stonewalling but I am still going to measure my words. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T345">“</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T346">I created an opportunity and guided Noah through it. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T348">Randel wandered off on his own. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T346">As for the forest that’s beyond my control. The trees are growing souls and learning magic. I assume that I inspired them but their actions are their own.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P63"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T348">And what do you mean by inspire?” he asks with narrowed eyes.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P63"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T348">The forest seems to be copying my decoys. I’ve never seen a tree learn to make illusions before but I guess they learned by example.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P63"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T348">And you have no idea how ‘they’ did this?”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P63"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T349">They grew souls around my illusions. I didn’t even know that was possible, but the System creates unique opportunities.” I might have said too much. The gears of Tarrick’s mind begin spinning and a few seconds later he is glaring at me. Stonewalling would have been the smart strategy but I’m not here for smart strategies. “Have you seen anything like it?”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P63"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T349">Putting human souls in trees falls under necromancy. A highly regulated field. Where did you get enough for an entire forest?” Tarrick demands. Alright soft manipulations are done.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P63"><text:soft-page-break/><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T349">It’s not necromancy if there’s no dead things, but what are you talking about? They’re definitely tree souls and souls just grow. Magic loves sticking to living things. I don’t even know why you guys are so worried about a harmless forest.” He blames me for murder and kidnapping but mischievous trees are his focus? I will never understand the thinking of imperials.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P63"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T350">You’ve unleashed some kind of soul plague and you don’t know why I am concerned!?” shouts Tarrick.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P63"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T351">A what? The trees are just growing. If you leave them alone they’ll just do their own thing.”</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P63"><text:tab/>“<text:span text:style-name="T351">Like I’ll believe that!” </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T352">Tarrick says. The rest of the conversation makes it clear that he has decided that there is a soul plague and reality can shut up if it disagrees. At no point does he even explain what a soul plague is beyond bad and spooky. These guys will pick a fight with anything but what a surprise for trees to be their first choice.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P63"><text:tab/><text:span text:style-name="T352">Meditation resumes when I get back to my cell. So far nothing has actually worked but my understanding has increased. I already knew magic is not physical but feeling a poke from an emotional direction is truly weird. Trying to shapeshift into that direction was even weirder. It’s clear there is enough room to add an illusion but my soul rejects external magic even if it’s from me. That’s not something I’m willing to break with force if I even could.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:h text:style-name="P106" text:outline-level="1">Chapter 13</text:h>
  </office:text>
 </office:body>
</office:document>